WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.830
When working with databases,

2
00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.320
you'll often have to add
new rows and columns to

3
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.455
existing tables or even create
new tables from scratch.

4
00:00:07.455 --> 00:00:10.485
For example, if you run
a college database,

5
00:00:10.485 --> 00:00:13.200
you'll have to add new rows
for every new student.

6
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.270
With SQL, you can perform

7
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.555
these tasks quickly using
the insert statement.

8
00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:20.355
By the end of this video,

9
00:00:20.355 --> 00:00:23.010
you'll be able to identify
and understand insert

10
00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.440
SQL syntax and insert data into

11
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.605
tables with the use of
the insert into clause.

12
00:00:28.605 --> 00:00:30.705
Let's begin with an exploration

13
00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:32.565
of the Insert into Syntax.

14
00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:34.575
To write an insert statement,

15
00:00:34.575 --> 00:00:36.270
first write an insert into

16
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.410
clause then specify the
table name followed

17
00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.480
by a list of columns
contained within

18
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.485
a pair of parentheses
and separated by commas.

19
00:00:44.485 --> 00:00:46.790
Then use the values
keyword and write

20
00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.280
a list of values within
a pair of parenthesis.

21
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.220
It's important to remember
that each value corresponds to

22
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.990
a specific column and so should

23
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.395
reflect the same
datatype and order.

24
00:00:56.395 --> 00:00:58.445
You can also add multiple rows

25
00:00:58.445 --> 00:01:00.155
into a table at the same time.

26
00:01:00.155 --> 00:01:01.880
First, write the insert

27
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.420
into clause and table,
just like before.

28
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.260
Then use the values keyword

29
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.030
and add multiple rows of values.

30
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.860
Just make sure that
each new row is

31
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.485
separated from a
previous one by a comma.

32
00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:15.965
Now let's explore some examples
of an insert statement.

33
00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:18.230
In this example,
I'll use a table

34
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.945
called players from a
sports club database.

35
00:01:20.945 --> 00:01:24.445
I want to insert new player
data into this table.

36
00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:26.510
First I write the insert into

37
00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.820
command followed by
the name of the table.

38
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.835
In this case, it's players.

39
00:01:30.835 --> 00:01:34.370
Then I add the column names
within a pair of parenthesis.

40
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.080
These columns must contain

41
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.340
the basic information that

42
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.530
the club requires
about each player.

43
00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:41.930
I'll name the columns: ID,

44
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.995
Name, Age, and Start date.

45
00:01:44.995 --> 00:01:47.210
Next, I insert the
values keyword

46
00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:48.290
and then add the values I

47
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.000
want to assign to each column

48
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.910
within a pair of parenthesis.

49
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.550
I start adding the data for
a new player named Yuval,

50
00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:01.200
age 25 with an ID of 1 and
a start date of 2020/10/15.

51
00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.835
It's important to use the
correct format of year,

52
00:02:04.835 --> 00:02:07.610
month, and day when
entering dates in a table.

53
00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.030
Otherwise, an error
message will appear.

54
00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.800
I can also use the current
date function followed

55
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.395
by a pair of empty parentheses
next to my values,

56
00:02:15.395 --> 00:02:16.895
just like I've done
for the new player,

57
00:02:16.895 --> 00:02:20.335
Yuval and end up scripted
all values for Yuval.

58
00:02:20.335 --> 00:02:23.335
Note that each value relates
to a specific column.

59
00:02:23.335 --> 00:02:24.980
Number 1 corresponds to

60
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.965
the player ID column:
Yuval to player name,

61
00:02:27.965 --> 00:02:29.765
25 to player age,

62
00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:31.520
and the date to start date.

63
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.860
This means that the
order in which I type

64
00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.730
my values within the
parentheses is very important.

65
00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.390
Otherwise, I might accidentally

66
00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.540
store these values in
the wrong columns.

67
00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:42.290
It's also important to note that

68
00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.410
all non-numeric values are
placed within quotation marks,

69
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.305
just like Yuval and the date
value in the statement.

70
00:02:48.305 --> 00:02:50.895
Finally, I execute
the statement.

71
00:02:50.895 --> 00:02:53.975
The output now shows one
row of data for Yuval,

72
00:02:53.975 --> 00:02:55.715
just as my code instructed.

73
00:02:55.715 --> 00:02:57.350
But what if I wanted to insert

74
00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.660
multiple records of
data into the table?

75
00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.480
Let's say that two new
players have joined the team.

76
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.905
The first player is Mark,

77
00:03:03.905 --> 00:03:09.455
age 27 with an ID of 2 and
a start date of 2020/10/12.

78
00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:12.410
The second player
is Karl, age 26,

79
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.165
with an ID of 3 and a
start date of 2020/10/07.

80
00:03:16.165 --> 00:03:20.405
Both Mark and Karl must be
inserted into the database.

81
00:03:20.405 --> 00:03:21.995
As you learned earlier,

82
00:03:21.995 --> 00:03:24.235
this is a very
straightforward task.

83
00:03:24.235 --> 00:03:27.005
First, I write the
insert into command,

84
00:03:27.005 --> 00:03:29.600
then I write the
table name players.

85
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.000
Next, I type the ID, name,

86
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.050
age, and start

87
00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.480
date columns within a
pair of parenthesis.

88
00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.280
Then I write the values keyword

89
00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.005
and insert two records of data.

90
00:03:39.005 --> 00:03:41.030
These data records
are contained within

91
00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.880
two pairs of parentheses
separated by a comma,

92
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.100
one for Mark and
another for Karl.

93
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.400
Finally, I run or
execute the statement.

94
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.350
I then check my
output which shows

95
00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.635
that all three players
are now in the table.

96
00:03:53.635 --> 00:03:57.245
So far I've explored how
to add data to the table.

97
00:03:57.245 --> 00:03:59.690
But it's also possible
to show existing data in

98
00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.085
the player table by executing
the following SQL query.

99
00:04:03.085 --> 00:04:06.860
First, I try to select clause
followed by an asterisk.

100
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.050
This asterisk tells SQL to

101
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.495
return all columns
within the table.

102
00:04:11.495 --> 00:04:15.250
Then I type the "From" keyword
and the name of the table.

103
00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.780
I execute the statement
and the output

104
00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.435
shows all data available
from the player table.

105
00:04:20.435 --> 00:04:22.640
You can now identify
and make sense of

106
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.965
the insert syntax as well as

107
00:04:24.965 --> 00:04:26.690
insert new data into tables with

108
00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:30.370
the insert into
clause. Good luck.